    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Noble Carbon will show how it’s making EV charger installs painless at TechCrunch Disrupt",
      "d": "Noble Carbon has developed a smart circuit breaker that allows households to electrify without the pain of a main panel upgrade.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/noble-carbon-will-show-how-its-making-ev-charger-installs-painless-at-techcrunch-disrupt/",
      "m": "09. 22. PM 03:00",
      "ts": 1790089200000,
      "fullText": "Plenty of people want to buy electric vehicles, but not everyone is prepared to shell out for an upgrade to their home’s electrical service just to add an EV charger. The worry is reasonable. One study found that upgrading things like electrical panels (that box in the garage or basement that delivers power throughout a home) can more than double charger installation costs. Rather than rip out the old panel, Noble Carbon has developed circuit breakers that can take the headache out of installing an EV charger, or a heat pump or an induction stove for that matter. The Pittsfield, Massachusetts, startup is one of 200 startups in this year&#8217;s TechCrunch Startup Battlefield , a pitch competition at TechCrunch Disrupt in San Francisco in October in which 20 finalists will present on the main stage. Noble Carbon’s breakers can adjust how much electricity they deliver to an EV charger in real time, allowing the chargers to squeeze into otherwise at-capacity panels. Electrical panels in people’s homes are typically designed to handle absolute peak usage, something that might happen only “a handful of hours per year,” Spencer Fields, Noble Carbon’s head of marketing, told TechCrunch. For example, if someone is hosting family for a holiday, they might find themselves simultaneously using the water heater, air conditioning, oven, and several TVs, all while every light is on in the house. The rest of the time, the electrical panel has room to spare. Jared Lebos explains how Noble Carbon&#8217;s breakers work to Massachusetts Gov. Maura Healey Image Credits: Josh Qualls, Director of Photography, Office of Governor Healey In that scenario, Noble Carbon’s smart breakers would turn down the power flowing to the devices they control, essentially guaranteeing that the entire house doesn’t draw more power than the panel can provide. “In order to avoid a main panel upgrade, you really only need control on your three to six largest loads [the biggest power-drawing appliances],” Fie"
    },
